window.docContents[138].resolve({"/Output-Formats/#html-conventions-math":{"contents":"TeX-style mathematical notation (written $`f(x)` or $$`f(x)`, and represented by the Inline.math constructor) is rendered to a <code> element with the class math, together with the class inline or display according to the requested mode.\nThe element's text content is the TeX code, which is not processed while generating HTML.\nFor example, $`\\frac{1}{2}` is represented in HTML as <code class=\"math inline\">\\frac{1}{2}</code>.\n\nMath is typeset in the browser using the bundled KaTeX library.\nWhen a page has loaded, the script in Html.math.js renders every element with these classes.\nPages that contain mathematical notation should include this script together with KaTeX itself: its stylesheet (Html.katex.css), its code (Html.katex.js), and its fonts (Html.katexFonts).\nThe stylesheet refers to the fonts by relative paths, so the file layout described in their docstrings should be preserved.\nThe helper function Html.writeKaTeX writes all of these files to a directory with that layout and should be called by the code that writes a genre's HTML.\n\nThe minified KaTeX CSS file's contents. The stylesheet loads the fonts in {name}katexFonts by relative paths.\n\nThe minified KaTeX JS file's contents.\n\nThe KaTeX font files. Keys are filenames of the form fonts/..., relative to the KaTeX stylesheet.\n\nA short script that renders all Verso math using KaTeX.\n\nWrites KaTeX's script, stylesheet, and fonts, along with {name}math.js, to {name}dir.The files are {lit}katex.css, {lit}katex.js, and {lit}math.js. The fonts are in the\n{lit}fonts subdirectory, where the stylesheet expects them.\n\n","context":"Writing Documentation in Lean with Verso\u0009Output Formats\u0009HTML\u0009Conventions","header":"5.1.1.3. Math","id":"/Output-Formats/#html-conventions-math"}});